- the medians of the day-29 and tick advisor hooks add up to 1.66 → 2.15 ms (dense1m) and 1.86 → 2.98 (bot256).
The tick saves only 0.2–0.3 ms at the median and about 2 ms at the p90 in return. The 62.9 ms predict (dense1m d11039) fell in the month where all five dense1m prototype runs predicted 2 places and used 1, a systematic waste; the base's tick that month (d11040) cost 2.7–4.4 ms. The base has the same class of spike on a tick (bot256 pristine_2, d21690, 62.9 ms), so A1 moves that risk to day 29, and a wasted prediction can add a spike the base would never have. Before adopting A1: (a) name the rule wasted at d11040 and the filter term that differs. d11040 is a demographics-sample tick, so re-test with P3. (b) Adopt A1 only if, with it, day 29's per-month worst and p90 stay ≤ the tick's bar after B3; otherwise ship T2 + P3 without A1.

R4 — Replace the pooled p90 (6 runs × 8 months) with a statistic that does not hinge on one fixture month. The headless runs are deterministic (the h_ runs end in identical states), so the 48 samples are 8 month states replicated. The pooled p90 is in effect the worst month: in dense1m frames it is d11040 in every run, the demographics-sample tick with a costly lookup. Proposal:
- take each month's median over the ≥3 runs per side;
- time ≥12 warm months (360 days after the warm-up, which also covers a year tick);
- gate the worst warm month ≤ +10 ms and print the per-month table.
On the design's own model, the worst warm month after T2 is: dense1m design unit 8.5, dense1m frames unit 10.0, dense1m month frame 10.9, bot256 design unit 7.4, bot256 frames unit 7.0, bot256 month frame 5.9. So A1 + P3 must carry the dense1m frames cells, and the report should say so.

